oe:use_brewing_distilling__doc_cocculus_indicus__q1 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Cocculus Indicus used in Brewing, Malting & Distilling: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 227"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "COCCULUS INDICUS: a poisonous berry, too frequently mixed with malt liquors, in order to make them intoxicating; but this practice is expressly forbidden by act of parliament, 12 Ann. c. 2. and 56 G. 3. c. 58, which latter statute prohibits druggists from even selling to brewers this and other noxious drugs, under very severe penalties. Fishermen have a mode..."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 227" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n227> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

oe:use_brewing_distilling__doc_cocculus_indicus__q2 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Cocculus Indicus used in Brewing, Malting & Distilling: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. COCCULUS INDICUS"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "COCCULUS INDICUS: or Indian berry, is the fruit of the Menispermum Cocculus, a large tree, which grows upon the coasts of Malabar, Ceylon, &c. The fruit is blackish, and of the size of a large pea. It owes its narcotic and poisonous qualities to the vegeto-alkaline chemical principle called picrotoxia, of which it contains about one-fiftieth part of its weig..."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. COCCULUS INDICUS" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

