oe:use_calico_printing__1304__q1 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Bark Quercitron used in Calico Cloth & Calico Printing: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. QUERCITRON"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "QUERCITRON: is the bark of the Quercus nigra, or yellow oak, a tree which grows in North America, The colouring principle of this yellow dye-stuff has been called Quercitrin, by its discoverer Chevreul. It forms small pale yellow spangles, like those of Aurum musivum, has a faint acid reaction, is pretty soluble in alcohol, hardly in ether, and little in wat..."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. QUERCITRON" ;
