oe:use_leather_tanning__0109__q2 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Shumach used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 952"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "SUMACH: (GER. Schmack, Sumach. DU. Sumak, Smak. DA. Smak, Sumak. Sw. Sumack, Smack. FR. Sumac, Roure, Roux. IT. Sommaço. SP. Zumaque. PORT. Sumagre, Cumagre. RUS. Sumak. POL. Garbarskie drzewko, Maźnika. LAT. Rhus.) Common sumach (Rhus coriaria) is a shrub that grows naturally in Syria, Palestine, Spain, and Portugal; in the two last it is cultivated with gr..."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 952" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n952> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

oe:use_leather_tanning__0109__q3 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Shumach used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. SUMACH"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "SUMACH: (Eng. and Fr.; Schmack, Germ.); is the powder of the leaves, peduncles, and young branches of the Rhus coriaria, and Rhus cotinus, shrubs which grow in Hungary, the Bannat, and the Illyrian provinces. Both kinds contain tannin, with a little yellow colouring-matter, and are a good deal employed for tanning light-coloured leathers; but the first is th..."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. SUMACH" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

