oe:use_leather_tanning__0132__q1 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Valonia used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying: J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 1612"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "VALONIA: A species of acorn, forming a very considerable article of export from the Morea and the Levant. The more substance there is in the husk, or cup of the acorn, the better. It is of a bright drab colour, which it preserves so long as it is kept dry; any dampness injures it; as it then turns black, and loses both its strength and value. It is principal..."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 1612" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/dictionaryprac00mccu/page/n1612> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

oe:use_leather_tanning__0132__q2 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Valonia used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. VALONIA"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "VALONIA: is a kind of acorn, imported from the Levant and the Morea for the use of tanners, as the husk or cup contains abundance of tannin. The quantity imported for home consumption in 1836, was 80,511 cwts.; of which Turkey furnished 58,724, Italy and the Italian islands, 7209."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. VALONIA" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

oe:use_leather_tanning__0132__q3 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Valonia used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying: Simmonds (1892), Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products, scan p. 274"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "Under the heads of Cork, Quercitron, and Valonia, other products of oak will be found noticed. OAK-BARK, the bark of the oak which is largely used for tanning; the inner cortical of young trees being preferred, as containing a larger proportion of tannin."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Simmonds (1892), Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products, scan p. 274" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n274/mode/1up> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

oe:use_leather_tanning__0072 a oe:EndUseAssertion ;
    rdfs:label "Galls used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying"@en ;
