    rdfs:label "Evidence for Tallow used in Soap Manufacture: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. PINEY TALLOW"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "PINEY TALLOW: is a concrete fat obtained by boiling with water the fruit of the Vateria indica, a tree common upon the Malabar coast. It seems to be a substance intermediate between tallow and wax; partaking of the nature of stearine. It melts at 97-1/2° F., is white or yellowish, has a spec. grav. of 0·926; is saponified by alkalies, and forms excellent can..."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. PINEY TALLOW" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

oe:use_soap_manufacture__0022__q3 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Tallow used in Soap Manufacture: McCulloch (1880), Dictionary of Commerce, scan p. 1173"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "Similar facts may be stated of flax, while the number and variety of substitutes for tallow, as a means for supplying artificial light, and for the manufacture of soap, are even more striking."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "McCulloch (1880), Dictionary of Commerce, scan p. 1173" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/dictionaryprac00mccu/page/n1173/mode/1up> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

