oe:use_woollen_silk_dyeing__0471__q1 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Orchal used in Woollen, Silk & Worsted Dyeing: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 26"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "ARCHIL: a violet red paste prepared from various lichens, such as Roccella tinctoria and fuciformis. The most esteemed comes from the Canaries and the Cape de Verde islands; it is extensively used for dyeing silks and woollens, but, although it imparts a beautiful colour, it lacks permanence; other products are called persis and cudbear. See OBCHILLA."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 26" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n26> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

oe:use_woollen_silk_dyeing__0471__q2 a oe:EvidenceQuote ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Orchal used in Woollen, Silk & Worsted Dyeing: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. VIOLET DYE"@en ;
    oe:quoteText "Silk is dyed a fugitive violet with either archil or brazil wood; but a fine fast violet, first by a crimson with cochineal, without tartar or tin mordant, and after washing, it is dipped in the indigo vat."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. VIOLET DYE" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    oe:retrievalMode "direct_sentence" .

